# ===========================================================================
# 4. Power — a coal plant in the south-east, wired to the spine
# ===========================================================================
CoalPlant 21 12

# The conductor column. (21,11) touches the plant, (21,3) touches the spine
# road at (21,2), and a road conducts power, so the whole street is live.
PowerLine 21 11
PowerLine 21 10
PowerLine 21 9
PowerLine 21 8
PowerLine 21 7
PowerLine 21 6
PowerLine 21 5
PowerLine 21 4
PowerLine 21 3

# A spur west along y=10 that peters out in open country — a bare conductor
# run with nothing under it and nothing at the end of it.
PowerLine 20 10
PowerLine 19 10
PowerLine 18 10

# A wind turbine left deliberately OFF the network, in the south-west. An
# isolated power source is ordinary play and its wilderness penalty still
# counts, so the dump should show it doing exactly that and no more.
WindTurbine 0 12

# ===========================================================================
# 5. Water — a pump piped up to the spine
# ===========================================================================
WaterPump 22 9
WaterPipe 22 8 underground
WaterPipe 22 7 underground
WaterPipe 22 6 underground
WaterPipe 22 5 underground
WaterPipe 22 4 underground
WaterPipe 22 3 underground

# ===========================================================================
# 6. Civic footprints — 1×1 and 2×2
# ===========================================================================
# School coverage is a BFS along roads and zones — not a radius — so both
# schools sit one zone back from the high street rather than out in the field,
# and the education block dumps real numbers instead of zeroes.
# 2×2 elementary school at (10,4)–(11,5).
ElementarySchool 10 4
# 2×2 high school at (13,4)–(14,5).
HighSchool 13 4
# 2×2 park at (18,6)–(19,7).
ParkLarge 18 6
# 1×1 park at (20,6).
Park 20 6

# The lots that carry a line while they develop. The line has to go on before
# a lot builds — `Tool::PowerLine` refuses a developed tile — which is
# precisely why "a developed lot carrying a line" is a state worth pinning.
# Four lots rather than one so the case does not hinge on which lot the growth
# RNG happens to pick first.
PowerLine 11 1
PowerLine 12 1
PowerLine 13 1
PowerLine 14 1

# ===========================================================================
# 7. Let the city run
# ===========================================================================
tick 100

# ===========================================================================
# 8. The awkward states. Everything below is built into a LIVE city.
# ===========================================================================

# --- a hydro line over each kind of carriageway ---------------------------
# over a road
PowerLine 12 2
# over a rail
PowerLine 6 4
# over a level crossing of its own — road AND rail underlay on one tile. The
# two crossings on the spine are left bare deliberately: they are the pair the
# harness compares to prove build order has not leaked back onto the wire.
Rail 17 6
Road 17 6
PowerLine 17 6
# the other build order: line first, road across it
PowerLine 12 5
Road 12 5

# --- a hydro line on its own, and the two ways to spoil it ----------------
# a lone line on open ground
PowerLine 2 6
# a line demoted to its flag by a later regrade, with a pipe under it
PowerLine 3 6
TerraformRaise 3 6
WaterPipe 3 6 underground
# trees planted straight through a live line
PowerLine 4 6
Tree 4 6
# water brushed over a live line
PowerLine 5 6
Water 5 6

# --- a hydro line over a zone, in both build orders -----------------------
Commercial 7 6
PowerLine 7 6
PowerLine 8 6
Commercial 8 6

# --- pipes ----------------------------------------------------------------
# a pipe under a road
WaterPipe 13 2 underground
# a lone pipe, nothing above it
WaterPipe 2 7 underground
# a pipe under water
WaterPipe 4 9 underground
Water 4 9

# --- #198: the bulldozer is layer-scoped -----------------------------------
# A road over a buried pipe, on a tile of its own so the sequence below reads
# on its own dump lines rather than folding into "a pipe under a road" above.
# From the surface, bulldozing takes the road and leaves the pipe untouched;
# from underground it takes the pipe and leaves the road; and once the pipe
# is gone, bulldozing underground again finds nothing there — refused, and
# (unlike the old view-blind precedence) charged nothing.
Road 20 13
WaterPipe 20 13 underground
Bulldoze 20 13
Bulldoze 20 13 underground
refuse Bulldoze 20 13 underground

# --- terrain --------------------------------------------------------------
# a bulldozed lake is still a lake — open water carries nothing in any
# stratum, so since #198 the click is refused outright, charging nothing
refuse Bulldoze 1 9
# but paving a lake and razing the pavement leaves land — the cheapest
# regrade on the palette, and deliberate
Water 3 9
Road 3 9
Bulldoze 3 9

# --- what the bulldozer leaves behind -------------------------------------
# a 1×1 park razed. Under the strata this leaves bare ground; the v4 tree
# left a ghost `Park` that went on scoring +4.0 for ever.
Park 20 8
Bulldoze 20 8
# one click anywhere in a 2×2 footprint clears the whole thing
ParkLarge 2 10
Bulldoze 3 11

# ===========================================================================
# 9. Guards — commands that must be REFUSED, and their message
# ===========================================================================
# a structure over a road and a live line
refuse Park 12 2
# a zone over rail
refuse Residential 6 2
# anything over a live building
refuse Road 21 12
refuse PowerLine 10 4
# a regrade that would strand a live building
refuse TerraformLower 21 12
# a tree over a live building
refuse Tree 0 12
# a footprint that runs off the edge of the map
refuse CoalPlant 23 15

# ===========================================================================
# 10. Run it out
# ===========================================================================
tick 300
